// Comparing things the language will not compare for you.
//
// A container that orders its keys needs to be told how, and one that hashes
// them needs to be told that too. Neither is something the compiler can do for
// an arbitrary `T`: there are no traits reaching this far -- an `impl<T>` does
// not yet scope its own parameter -- and the built-in `==` over a struct
// compares where the two *are* rather than what they hold, which is a wrong
// answer that looks like an answer.
//
// So the operations are values, handed to a container once when it is made,
// and this file holds the ones for the type most keys are.

// Strictly less. `a` and `b` are references and an operator over a reference
// is an operator over what it refers to (§3), so this is the comparison of the
// two i64 and not of the two addresses.
pub fn less_i64(a: &i64, b: &i64): bool {
    a < b
}

pub fn eq_i64(a: &i64, b: &i64): bool {
    a == b
}

// `splitmix64`'s finaliser: three shifts, two multiplies and no state.
//
// It matters more than a hash usually does, because the tables here probe
// *forward*: keys that hash near each other pile into one another's places, so
// the identity hash a counter would produce turns a table into a linear scan.
// This sends neighbouring keys somewhere unrelated, which is all that is asked
// of it -- nothing here is keeping a secret.
pub fn mix(k: &i64): i64 {
    // The first line reads the key and the rest work on what it read.
    var h = (k ^ (k >> 30)) * -4658895280553007687
    h = (h ^ (h >> 27)) * -7723592293110705685
    h ^ (h >> 31)
}
